1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,019
This is Liam, in for Bear. I don't write
code, and I'm about to build something

2
00:00:04,019 --> 00:00:08,140
anyway. That's what vibecoding is: you
describe the thing, Claude Code writes it,

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:12,261
you decide whether it's right. An empty
folder, one file in it, and one paragraph

4
00:00:12,261 --> 00:00:14,398
— every constraint I care about, up front.

5
00:00:14,398 --> 00:00:15,670
Watch what it does first.

6
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:20,008
Here's the idea of this film. Vibecoding
means you describe a tool in plain words,

7
00:00:20,008 --> 00:00:24,087
Claude Code writes it, and you decide
whether it's right — not by trusting it,

8
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:28,218
by reading it. I'll build a reading log
I'd actually use, and I'll show you the

9
00:00:28,218 --> 00:00:32,454
two things it added that I never asked
for, and the one thing it wrote outside my

10
00:00:32,454 --> 00:00:32,820
folder.

11
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:34,854
Five words before we start. Vibecoding:

12
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:38,670
you describe the tool in plain words,
Claude writes the code, you judge it.

13
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:42,689
Local storage: the browser's own small
store — the page keeps your data with no

14
00:00:42,689 --> 00:00:46,251
server. Tool call: Claude asking for
permission to read, write, or run

15
00:00:46,251 --> 00:00:50,270
something. Diff: the exact lines removed,
in red, and added, in green — nothing

16
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:54,137
else. And git diff stat: the change
tracker's one-line answer — which files,

17
00:00:54,137 --> 00:00:54,900
how many lines.

18
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:58,787
Before it writes, it says what it's going
to write. Listen to the end of that

19
00:00:58,787 --> 00:01:02,774
sentence: 'and a delete option.' I never
asked for a delete option. I'm going to

20
00:01:02,774 --> 00:01:06,711
let it write anyway — a two-line fix is
cheap — but I've just learned the first

21
00:01:06,711 --> 00:01:09,900
